    shortDescription: "Explain transactions and ACID.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA transaction groups database operations into an atomic unit. ACID refers to atomicity, consistency, isolation, and durability; the exact guarantees depend on the database and transaction configuration.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question checks whether you understand database behavior under real data volume and concurrency. A strong answer separates logical correctness from query or storage performance.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State the database concept or guarantee.\n2. Explain how the engine or schema uses it.\n3. Connect it to query plans, transactions, or data distribution where relevant.\n4. Mention the trade-off or failure case.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the database rule first, then explain what happens to the data or query plan. I would add a concrete SQL or transaction example and mention what changes when the data size or concurrency grows.\n\n**Example :**\nA money transfer can debit one account and credit another in the same transaction so a failure does not leave only one side applied.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nTransactions protect correctness but can increase locking, contention, and resource usage if held longer than necessary.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Assuming an index or database feature is always beneficial.\n- Ignoring NULLs, duplicates, transactions, isolation, or query plans where they matter.\n- Giving a correctness claim without stating the database behavior that supports it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the key idea first, then justify it with the invariant, constraint, or decision rule that makes the answer correct.",
