    focus: "distributed systems, real-time services, geospatial systems, event-driven architecture, APIs, and reliability",
    question: "Design a URL Shortener",
    slug: "uber-design-a-url-shortener-81",
    shortDescription: "Design a scalable URL-shortening service.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA URL shortener maps a short identifier to a long URL, with a write path that creates the mapping and a read path that resolves the identifier. The design must consider ID generation, persistence, redirects, caching, abuse, and scale.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks architecture judgment: requirements, bottlenecks, failure modes, consistency, and operational cost. The best answer explains why the components fit together instead of naming many services.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Clarify the core functional requirement.\n2. State the most important scale and reliability assumptions.\n3. Describe the request or data flow.\n4. Identify the bottleneck, failure mode, and key trade-off.\n5. Explain how you would validate the design.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would start with the requirement and the assumption that drives the architecture. Then I would walk through the main request or data flow, call out the likely bottleneck and failure mode, and finish with how I would measure the design in production.\n\n**Example :**\nCreate a short code such as `aZ91`, store it with the destination URL, and have GET `/aZ91` return a redirect.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nThe read path is usually much hotter than the write path, so caching and horizontally scalable redirect handling are important design considerations.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Drawing components before stating requirements.\n- Claiming that a system “scales” without identifying what limits it.\n- Ignoring consistency, retries, idempotency, observability, or operational ownership.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the key idea first, then justify it with the invariant, constraint, or decision rule that makes the answer correct.",
